---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import { sections } from '../lib/sections';

const markSvg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 64 64" role="img" aria-label="othala rune">
  <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4.5" stroke-linecap="round">
    <line x1="32" y1="8" x2="48" y2="24"/>
    <line x1="48" y1="24" x2="32" y2="40"/>
    <line x1="32" y1="40" x2="16" y2="24"/>
    <line x1="16" y1="24" x2="32" y2="8"/>
    <line x1="32" y1="40" x2="18" y2="56"/>
    <line x1="32" y1="40" x2="46" y2="56"/>
  </g>
  <circle cx="32" cy="24" r="2.5" fill="currentColor"/>
</svg>`;

// Entry points below the story: Architecture is a standalone page (not
// a content-collection section, see lib/sections.ts), Guides comes from
// `sections` so it stays in sync with the one place that lists them,
// and Source is the repo this site is a reading copy of.
const guides = sections.flatMap(s => s.pages).filter(p => p.file).length;
const diagrams = readdirSync(resolve(process.cwd(), 'public/diagrams')).filter(f => f.endsWith('.svg')).length;
const decisions = (readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), '../DECISIONS.md'), 'utf-8').match(/^## /gm) ?? []).length;
const stats = [
  { num: guides, label: 'guides' },
  { num: diagrams, label: 'diagrams' },
  { num: decisions, label: 'decisions' },
];

const entryPoints = [
  {
    href: '/architecture/',
    label: 'Architecture',
    desc: 'How Kratos, Hydra, the portal, and the edge fit together, told through six diagrams and the reasoning between them.',
  },
  ...sections.map(s => ({ href: s.href, label: s.label, desc: s.lede })),
  {
    href: 'https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/othala',
    label: 'Source',
    desc: 'The code, the tests, and the decisions log this site is a reading copy of.',
    external: true,
  },
];
---
<Layout title="Home" description="Othala is one family's home single sign-on, built on Ory Kratos and Hydra with a TypeScript portal and a Caddy forward-auth endpoint. Public so it can be read and copied. Architecture, guides, and decisions from a single-maintainer homelab.">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={markSvg} />
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">dunn.dev</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title">Othala</h1>
    <p class="hero-lede">
      Othala is the single sign-on for one family's house: Ory Kratos and
      Hydra behind a TypeScript portal that supplies the login provider,
      consent provider, and access gate they ship without, plus a Caddy
      forward-auth endpoint for the apps that speak no OIDC at all.
      Passkey-first, running for a handful of people. This site is the
      reading copy: an architecture walkthrough of why it is wired the way
      it is, the guides for how, and the decisions behind both.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="stat-strip" data-reveal>
    {stats.map(s => (
      <div><span class="stat-num">{s.num}</span><span class="stat-label">{s.label}</span></div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-subtitle">Start here</p>
    <div class="tile-grid" data-reveal>
      {entryPoints.map(e => (
        <a href={e.href} class="tile" rel={e.external ? 'external' : undefined}>
          <h2 class="tile-headline">{e.label}{e.external ? ' ↗' : ''}</h2>
          <p class="tile-prose">{e.desc}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="section-prose">
      It is public because a deployment with no support contract has nothing
      to lose by being readable, and because most of what makes an Ory
      integration hard has no complete worked example in the open. This one
      does, with the reasoning kept next to the code. Read it, copy it, lift
      a file out whole. It is not a pitch: no roadmap, no adoption path, one
      maintainer.
    </p>
    <p class="section-prose">
      Every application it fronts is one manifest file: what the tile says,
      what OAuth2 client Hydra sees, and which group grants entry versus
      which one grants a role. Adding an app is a file drop, not an image
      rebuild.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

